   "subject": "Re: [r-inla] prior of Poisson GLMM",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "\r\n<span>We recommend the PC priors we introduced here: <a href=\"http://arxiv.org/abs/1403.4630\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://arxiv.org/abs/1403.4630&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792446056689000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0e-lTQo380I200m_NSZyjn\">http://arxiv.org/abs/1403.4630</a><div><br></div>\r\n<div>It&#39;s implemented as pc.prec</div>\r\n<div><br></div>\r\n<div>D</div></span><div>—<br>Sent from <a href=\"https://www.dropbox.com/mailbox\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www.dropbox.com/mailbox&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792446056689000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1UNw-cKA_5mj2vY0C-GzSk\">Mailbox</a>\r\n</div>\r\n<br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><p>On Fri, Sep 26, 2014 at 10:36 AM, Martí Casals <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">marti...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></p></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\">\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-family:arial,sans-serif;line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:9pt;font-family:Arial;color:black;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\"><br></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-family:arial,sans-serif;line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:9pt;font-family:Arial;color:black;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\">Dear INLA help,</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-family:arial,sans-serif;line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:9pt;font-family:Arial;color:black;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\"> </span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-family:arial,sans-serif;line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:9pt;font-family:Arial\"><br></span><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-family:&#39;Arial Unicode MS&#39;\"></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-family:arial,sans-serif;line-height:normal;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:9pt;font-family:Arial;color:black;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\">I fitted a Poisson mixed model with a classical random effect (“iid”). The model is a little bit overdispersed. Then,</span><span lang=\"EN-US\"> </span><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:9pt;font-family:Arial;color:black;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\">I want to fit a classical Poisson GLMM with offset in inla. In the paper of Fong et al (2009)  they don&#39;t recommend the “<b>param=c(0.001,0.001)</b>” prior. Thus, I’d like to know which you would use. </span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-family:arial,sans-serif;line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:9pt;font-family:Arial;color:black;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\"> </span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-family:arial,sans-serif;line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:9pt;font-family:Arial;color:black;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\"><br>In a recent paper of L. Grilli (2014) entiled ``Bayesian estimation with integrated nested Laplace approximation for binary logit mixed models&#39;&#39;, they didn’t use an inla default prior because it is very flat, and finally they used Γ (a=0.5,b=0.0164) for logistic mixed model.<br> </span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-family:arial,sans-serif;line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:9pt;font-family:Arial;color:black;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\">My question is, which prior  would you recommend me to fit a poisson mixed model?</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-family:arial,sans-serif;line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:9pt;font-family:Arial;color:black;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\"> </span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px;font-family:arial,sans-serif;line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:9pt;font-family:Arial;color:black;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\">I think the following one could be good, but I’m not sure:</span></p>\r\n<span style=\"color:rgb(80,0,80);font-family:arial,sans-serif\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:9pt;font-family:Arial;color:black;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\"><br> hyper.iid &lt;- list(theta=list(prior=&quot;logtgaussian&quot;,<b>param=c(0,0.0001)</b>))<br><br></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:9pt;font-family:Arial;color:black;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\"> </span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:9pt;font-family:Arial;color:black;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\">Thanks in advance.</span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:9pt;font-family:Arial;color:black;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\"><br></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0px\"><span lang=\"EN-US\" style=\"font-size:9pt;font-family:Arial;color:black;background-image:initial;background-repeat:initial\">Martí</span></p></span>\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n<p></p></blockquote></div>"
